DESCRIPTION:<u>Title:</u> <span>Entry and Acquisitions in Software Markets
 </span><br> <br><u>Abstract:</u> <span>How do acquisitions of young\, in
 novative\, venture capital-funded firms (startups) affect incentives to en
 ter a market? I construct a novel product-level dataset of enterprise soft
 ware\, and apply text-as-data methods to identify competing firms. I docum
 ent new empirical patterns on startup acquisitions in software\, including
  who acquires whom\, and how these acquisitions relate to subsequent entry
 . To interpret these patterns and to quantify the underlying mechanisms\, 
 I build and estimate a dynamic modelof startup entry in the face of these 
 acquisitions. In the model\, acquisitions influence returns to entry (1) b
 y affecting market structure\, and (2) by creating an entry-for-buyout inc
 entive for potential entrants. Using thee stimated model\, I simulate how 
 entry would evolve under alternative merger policies. The simulations reve
 al that\, if all startup acquisitions were blocked\, entry would decline b
 y about 16% in the average market. By contrast\, blocking acquisitions of 
 more mature startups by established incumbents would increase entry slight
 ly. These results indicate that case-by-case merger reviewis best suited t
 o sustain startup entry.</span>
LAST-MODIFIED:20251111T094010Z
LOCATION:OMDS Seminar Room
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
