DESCRIPTION:<span><u>Title:</u> </span><span>Climate Change Resilience th
 rough Health Systems: TheRole of Healthcare Access in Shielding Children f
 rom Drought-RelatedUndernutrition in sub-Saharan Africa</span><br><span><b
 r><u>Abstract:</u> </span><span>Climate change is increasing the frequenc
 y andseverity of droughts with particularly acute consequences for populat
 ions insub-Saharan Africa. We investigate whether access to healthcare can
  mitigate the nutritional impacts of drought on young children by assembli
 ng a geospatial dataset that links Demographic and Health Surveys from 34 
 countries (1986–2021) with high-resolution drought indices and modeled t
 ravel times to health facilities. We focus on exposure during gestation an
 d early childhood to estimate effects on height-for-age and weight-for-hei
 ght. By exploring the moderating role of healthcare accessibility\, our st
 udy contributes new evidence on health system strengthening as a climate r
 esilience strategy.</span>
LAST-MODIFIED:20250916T093918Z
LOCATION:OMDS Seminar Room
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
