SEQUENCE:0
CREATED:20250814T111222Z
DESCRIPTION:<u>Title:</u> <span>Flooding in Informal Settlements Evidence
  from Kibera</span><br><br><u>Abstract:</u> <span>Informal settlements fo
 rm in the margins of cities\, in land left unoccupied by formal planning a
 nd construction\, which is often at high risk from environmental hazards\,
  including flooding. A key question for policy-makers is to what extent re
 sidents of informal settlements understand these risks and choose to live 
 in these neighbourhoods anyway\, given a limited set of alternatives\, or 
 whether imperfect information leads people to expose themselves to risks t
 hey do not understand. We study housing markets in the context of the larg
 e informal settlement of Kibera\, in Nairobi\, Kenya.  We use rich and no
 vel data to describe sorting along flood risk: high-flood-risk properties 
 are rented by residents with shorter tenancy durations who report greater 
 flood-related damages\, while rents change non-monotonically with flood ri
 sk. These patterns are consistent with some degree of information asymmetr
 y about flood risk between property owners and tenants. This study sheds l
 ight on the root causes of exposure to environmental hazards in informal s
 ettlements. </span>
LAST-MODIFIED:20251118T063531Z
LOCATION:OMDS Seminar Room
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
